    ``` sudo ufw allow 80,443/tcp   # allow HTTP
    ``` sudo ufw limit 22/tcp       # limit means limited number of connection retries
    ``` sudo ufw default deny incoming
    ``` sudo ufw default allow outgoing
    ``` sudo ufw enable


Recommendations
* close unused open ports
* If you SSH into your computer, maybe use other port than 22, so the default port isn't open (hackers would check it by default)
    * make sure ssh connection is done with proper validation (key file)
    * shouldn't permit root-login (config file: /etc/ssh/sshd_config )

* log connection attempts
    * also enable rejections (install "fail2ban", and configure/enable it)
    * also enable alerts, so you know if something happened and can act accordingly
* disable unused services, such as printing, or Bluetooth (specifically problematic regardless)
    * check all your systemd services

* Make sure History doesn't contain sensitive information (passwords, addresses)

* Update your software (have the versions that resolve CVEs)
    * consider removing libraries you're not using

Firmware (from linux.conf.au: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gP_9sUfpW_o )
* Run latest firmware
    * Management Engine before 12 has no rollback protection
    * Linux Vendor Firmware Service (LVFS) provides consistent interface
* Reduce attack surface via firmware
    * Linuxboot - payload for existing UEFI PEI layers
    * Yabits - payload for Coreboot, small UEFI implementations
    * Project Mu - derived from Tiano





